m and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-160bbd50-69e6-5813-9152-8fb571501011"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-160bbd50-69e6-5813-9152-8fb571501011" :answer-field/id #uuid "160bbd50-69e6-5813-9152-8fb571501011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-358ce659-62c7-5b50-8b6a-72ee4e8e55df" "answer-3ec18b62-e688-5729-8acb-74067dce2afa" "answer-e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" "answer-b575e07e-689b-50db-9fe4-49b41ddcdf6b" "answer-070e71d3-4fa0-5f24-95c6-4392df98055f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-358ce659-62c7-5b50-8b6a-72ee4e8e55df" :answer/id #uuid "358ce659-62c7-5b50-8b6a-72ee4e8e55df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ec18b62-e688-5729-8acb-74067dce2afa" :answer/id #uuid "3ec18b62-e688-5729-8acb-74067dce2afa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" :answer/id #uuid "e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b575e07e-689b-50db-9fe4-49b41ddcdf6b" :answer/id #uuid "b575e07e-689b-50db-9fe4-49b41ddcdf6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-070e71d3-4fa0-5f24-95c6-4392df98055f" :answer/id #uuid "070e71d3-4fa0-5f24-95c6-4392df98055f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c0c2f91-9457-576f-ab5b-77c8f4a530ad" :question/id #uuid "5c0c2f91-9457-576f-ab5b-77c8f4a530ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-350579" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77d5f4a7-be7e-5df2-a989-37fce15016eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77d5f4a7-be7e-5df2-a989-37fce15016eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "77d5f4a7-be7e-5df2-a989-37fce15016eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86" :answer/id #uuid "fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-56e0e8e1-0cfd-5d25-b2ed-327a18d60529" :question/id #uuid "56e0e8e1-0cfd-5d25-b2ed-327a18d60529" :question/math-academy-id "q-350582" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}}{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-\\frac{3}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{5}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d13a95c4-8252-5e9e-aaed-c7750144cf3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d13a95c4-8252-5e9e-aaed-c7750144cf3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d13a95c4-8252-5e9e-aaed-c7750144cf3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951" :answer/id #uuid "df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e8589d7-77a6-5ea2-bd7d-7f7a24b35382" :question/id #uuid "7e8589d7-77a6-5ea2-bd7d-7f7a24b35382" :question/math-academy-id "q-78588" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c3c0ce87-8111-5c96-ab4f-9edca6a3f6b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c3c0ce87-8111-5c96-ab4f-9edca6a3f6b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c0ce87-8111-5c96-ab4f-9edca6a3f6b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18efe5ab-4593-5cd8-931e-6b73131d825c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9b16bc4-347a-513f-ac2b-06d7b3a9de9c" "answer-edbf054f-cc52-584e-8129-fbfe69dad2a0" "answer-7f4ef9fd-0ecb-5af4-9f89-3aa00d5a46be" "answer-5477a5df-1c13-5533-ac14-d6f0af484e34" "answer-18efe5ab-4593-5cd8-931e-6b73131d825c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9b16bc4-347a-513f-ac2b-06d7b3a9de9c" :answer/id #uuid "e9b16bc4-347a-513f-ac2b-06d7b3a9de9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edbf054f-cc52-584e-8129-fbfe69dad2a0" :answer/id #uuid "edbf054f-cc52-584e-8129-fbfe69dad2a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f4ef9fd-0ecb-5af4-9f89-3aa00d5a46be" :answer/id #uuid "7f4ef9fd-0ecb-5af4-9f89-3aa00d5a46be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5477a5df-1c13-5533-ac14-d6f0af484e34" :answer/id #uuid "5477a5df-1c13-5533-ac14-d6f0af484e34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18efe5ab-4593-5cd8-931e-6b73131d825c" :answer/id #uuid "18efe5ab-4593-5cd8-931e-6b73131d825c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd479934-e321-5035-bf71-51bd7984a9a8" :question/id #uuid "dd479934-e321-5035-bf71-51bd7984a9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124588" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}=\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{6})}{5}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5101b7ce-f6ad-523b-93e4-83e6665f500b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5101b7ce-f6ad-523b-93e4-83e6665f500b" :answer-field/id #uuid "5101b7ce-f6ad-523b-93e4-83e6665f500b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c4e7ff3-16b6-51f5-a98c-6ce0c3972663" "answer-843d0574-e70b-5e22-b58c-6a661198e3c4" "answer-b2433a72-ac85-5f1f-b794-39e122d2d8d7" "answer-0ad32293-81c4-5632-9b56-dce7d9c78668" "answer-3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c4e7ff3-16b6-51f5-a98c-6ce0c3972663" :answer/id #uuid "5c4e7ff3-16b6-51f5-a98c-6ce0c3972663" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-843d0574-e70b-5e22-b58c-6a661198e3c4" :answer/id #uuid "843d0574-e70b-5e22-b58c-6a661198e3c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2433a72-ac85-5f1f-b794-39e122d2d8d7" :answer/id #uuid "b2433a72-ac85-5f1f-b794-39e122d2d8d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ad32293-81c4-5632-9b56-dce7d9c78668" :answer/id #uuid "0ad32293-81c4-5632-9b56-dce7d9c78668" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069" :answer/id #uuid "3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092790 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{64})}{(\\frac{1}{16})}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{16})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{16})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186090940 :answer-field/correct 17592186090945 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-54f15411-79a5-546c-8ee4-965cb1e88cd5" :question/id #uuid "54f15411-79a5-546c-8ee4-965cb1e88cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350607" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{16}{81} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5}=\\frac{32}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{32}{243})}{(\\frac{16}{81})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1cde64eb-fc8f-574e-a4b3-4a914b9a4d5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1cde64eb-fc8f-574e-a4b3-4a914b9a4d5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cde64eb-fc8f-574e-a4b3-4a914b9a4d5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7" :answer/id #uuid "976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d588b5a8-8a0c-5e4d-8e17-d8c93ee42445" :question/id #uuid "d588b5a8-8a0c-5e4d-8e17-d8c93ee42445" :question/math-academy-id "q-78592" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{9}{25})=\\frac{3}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{9}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{125})}{(\\frac{3}{25})}=\\frac{9}{125}\\cdot \\frac{25}{3}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec27395b-38a3-58fa-99e7-8f30382149a4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec27395b-38a3-58fa-99e7-8f30382149a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec27395b-38a3-58fa-99e7-8f30382149a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c31661b-2050-5fa7-bf5f-9b3a7ef311ae" "answer-94cc8691-117b-5651-83f2-f780d14663d8" "answer-2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" "answer-8559fe88-e67b-5f1b-bf3d-bbf3de7441ec" "answer-12bfe273-020f-5d83-9faf-6cff62334849"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c31661b-2050-5fa7-bf5f-9b3a7ef311ae" :answer/id #uuid "2c31661b-2050-5fa7-bf5f-9b3a7ef311ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94cc8691-117b-5651-83f2-f780d14663d8" :answer/id #uuid "94cc8691-117b-5651-83f2-f780d14663d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" :answer/id #uuid "2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8559fe88-e67b-5f1b-bf3d-bbf3de7441ec" :answer/id #uuid "8559fe88-e67b-5f1b-bf3d-bbf3de7441ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12bfe273-020f-5d83-9faf-6cff62334849" :answer/id #uuid "12bfe273-020f-5d83-9faf-6cff62334849" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-75a391e4-92f0-5306-8519-d804235fa403" :question/id #uuid "75a391e4-92f0-5306-8519-d804235fa403" :question/math-academy-id "q-78591" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{64}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{64}{125})}{(\\frac{16}{25})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(1-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c15c1c6b-8e24-5b94-b478-74191ff0b0e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c15c1c6b-8e24-5b94-b478-74191ff0b0e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "c15c1c6b-8e24-5b94-b478-74191ff0b0e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f2772e5-3a67-5a55-9efd-47b34d382cca" "answer-2105d49c-3838-5c6f-a36c-1a555b26bcd2" "answer-25c78422-62a4-52b1-bc72-b3de2ce6a592" "answer-0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" "answer-2a9324da-a375-57dd-a2cb-31842855799b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f2772e5-3a67-5a55-9efd-47b34d382cca" :answer/id #uuid "0f2772e5-3a67-5a55-9efd-47b34d382cca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2105d49c-3838-5c6f-a36c-1a555b26bcd2" :answer/id #uuid "2105d49c-3838-5c6f-a36c-1a555b26bcd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25c78422-62a4-52b1-bc72-b3de2ce6a592" :answer/id #uuid "25c78422-62a4-52b1-bc72-b3de2ce6a592" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" :answer/id #uuid "0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a9324da-a375-57dd-a2cb-31842855799b" :answer/id #uuid "2a9324da-a375-57dd-a2cb-31842855799b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4db8cfdc-3e22-5283-b109-341d9daf5b09" :question/id #uuid "4db8cfdc-3e22-5283-b109-341d9daf5b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350625" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(1)}=(-7)(0.3)^{2}=(-7)(0.09)=-0.63,\n$$\n\nand the second term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(2)}=(-7)(0.3)^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-7)(0.3)^{4}}{(-7)(0.3)^{2}}=(0.3)^{2}=0.09.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-0.63)}{1-0.09} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0.63}{0.91} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35c0bbe3-e9a9-52f6-ab2f-3984c5548c8c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35c0bbe3-e9a9-52f6-ab2f-3984c5548c8c" :answer-field/id #uuid "35c0bbe3-e9a9-52f6-ab2f-3984c5548c8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cea5f493-396d-519f-9baa-177ed893c00c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cea5f493-396d-519f-9baa-177ed893c00c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cea5f493-396d-519f-9baa-177ed893c00c" :answer/id #uuid "cea5f493-396d-519f-9baa-177ed893c00c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a6fcb51-44e7-59f8-9078-8afece3073a1" :question/id #uuid "7a6fcb51-44e7-59f8-9078-8afece3073a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-350624" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(0.5)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6(0.5)^{2(1)}=6(0.5)^{2}=6(0.25)=1.5,\n$$\n\nand the second term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=6(0.5)^{2(2)}=6(0.5)^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6(0.5)^{4}}{6(0.5)^{2}}=(0.5)^{2}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(0.5)^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{1-0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{0.75} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5b93b28c-ccc2-5022-a0b7-15dc266d4717"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5b93b28c-ccc2-5022-a0b7-15dc266d4717" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b93b28c-ccc2-5022-a0b7-15dc266d4717" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1af309f6-8280-5978-9ceb-4d701d91b214" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1af309f6-8280-5978-9ceb-4d701d91b214"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1af309f6-8280-5978-9ceb-4d701d91b214" :answer/id #uuid "1af309f6-8280-5978-9ceb-4d701d91b214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092540 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(0.5)^{4n}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3(0.5)^{4(1)}=3(0.5)^{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=3(0.5)^{4(2)}=3(0.5)^{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3(0.5)^{8}}{3(0.5)^{4}}=(0.5)^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(0.5)^{4n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(0.5)^{4}}{1-(0.5)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089411 :answer-field/correct 17592186089413 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-499dc119-15d3-5a33-9631-8bc1b2a65d1c" :question/id #uuid "499dc119-15d3-5a33-9631-8bc1b2a65d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-5362" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}}{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}(\\frac{4}{25})}{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}\\cdot \\frac{25}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0aaf7a48-2de5-5bcf-abf9-e9ddfe97ac48"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0aaf7a48-2de5-5bcf-abf9-e9ddfe97ac48" :answer-field/id #uuid "0aaf7a48-2de5-5bcf-abf9-e9ddfe97ac48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" "answer-04935b22-0a1e-541f-a73a-987f30ee4b47" "answer-2cbb7959-4b4b-548e-9199-2fcffd9bfefb" "answer-f008eb8a-552e-5329-9ff3-06c4e1005ae4" "answer-aaca8c15-d6ca-5c02-bf7f-dcb675eef426"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" :answer/id #uuid "1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04935b22-0a1e-541f-a73a-987f30ee4b47" :answer/id #uuid "04935b22-0a1e-541f-a73a-987f30ee4b47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cbb7959-4b4b-548e-9199-2fcffd9bfefb" :answer/id #uuid "2cbb7959-4b4b-548e-9199-2fcffd9bfefb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f008eb8a-552e-5329-9ff3-06c4e1005ae4" :answer/id #uuid "f008eb8a-552e-5329-9ff3-06c4e1005ae4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aaca8c15-d6ca-5c02-bf7f-dcb675eef426" :answer/id #uuid "aaca8c15-d6ca-5c02-bf7f-dcb675eef426" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093368 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate the infinite geometric series \n \n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1} ^ \\infty  3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4\\right) ^ n.\n$$\n\nThe sum $S_\\infty$ of an infinite geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_{\\infty} = \\dfrac {\\color{black}a_1} {1 - {\\color{black}r}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{black}r}$ is the common ratio and $\\color{black}a_1$ is the first term. So, we need to determine $\\color{black}a_1$ and $\\color{black}r$ and then plug those values into this formula.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{\\color{black}a_1} = 3 \\left( \\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1 = {\\color{black}\\dfrac 3 4},\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}r} = \\dfrac {a_2}{\\color{black}a_1} = \\dfrac {3 \\left (\\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^2}{3\\left( \\dfrac 1 4 \\right)^1}= {\\color{black}\\dfrac 1 4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092404 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186134402 :question/answer-fields 17592186132451] {:db/id 17592186133814 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6e2c74ef-5be6-50fe-9eda-c944716ab881" "question-01f92ee9-73cd-5c08-adfa-addc2317087e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133629 :question/problem "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 3x_3 y_3.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector ![](images/59/596d25646a513272f62708b9370136c1ef45a120edee3a4454c1a559119be3e5.svg) under this inner product." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133767 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7e56a686-12c8-5112-a821-3890332df74f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e2c74ef-5be6-50fe-9eda-c944716ab881" :question/id #uuid "6e2c74ef-5be6-50fe-9eda-c944716ab881" :question/math-academy-id "q-43906" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-1))+2\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+3+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c630f8e1-0324-5294-a68b-04d4d6edff9d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c630f8e1-0324-5294-a68b-04d4d6edff9d" :answer-field/id #uuid "c630f8e1-0324-5294-a68b-04d4d6edff9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b314f0bf-f9e4-5eb6-8317-c15e1e7bc15f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cb92e58e-5247-58b6-b945-2ce280bce11f" "answer-8801b776-a19a-596b-b075-b22735277384" "answer-b314f0bf-f9e4-5eb6-8317-c15e1e7bc15f" "answer-cd7fdef6-0c83-5dab-a365-d572940ee5d5" "answer-365ca546-2c5d-5019-a455-b4999bc2be24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb92e58e-5247-58b6-b945-2ce280bce11f" :answer/id #uuid "cb92e58e-5247-58b6-b945-2ce280bce11f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8801b776-a19a-596b-b075-b22735277384" :answer/id #uuid "8801b776-a19a-596b-b075-b22735277384" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b314f0bf-f9e4-5eb6-8317-c15e1e7bc15f" :answer/id #uuid "b314f0bf-f9e4-5eb6-8317-c15e1e7bc15f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd7fdef6-0c83-5dab-a365-d572940ee5d5" :answer/id #uuid "cd7fdef6-0c83-5dab-a365-d572940ee5d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-365ca546-2c5d-5019-a455-b4999bc2be24" :answer/id #uuid "365ca546-2c5d-5019-a455-b4999bc2be24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-01f92ee9-73cd-5c08-adfa-addc2317087e" :question/id #uuid "01f92ee9-73cd-5c08-adfa-addc2317087e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141165" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)+5\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (-2))+2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+5+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d3a6e30f-e49a-55e3-89a2-979eca32e275"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d3a6e30f-e49a-55e3-89a2-979eca32e275" :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a6e30f-e49a-55e3-89a2-979eca32e275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-20e15b4c-4991-5dc9-8b45-ed10cc092a7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3b898d0-2a91-50f3-ba29-21df42a1673f" "answer-20e15b4c-4991-5dc9-8b45-ed10cc092a7d" "answer-4a53b2d0-48eb-5228-9ea2-2000e2948d59" "answer-5aae46bc-e0c9-5b85-8008-b239c57b2c11" "answer-d1ffc7c4-55dc-5d3f-b3b3-8c34240ac735"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3b898d0-2a91-50f3-ba29-21df42a1673f" :answer/id #uuid "f3b898d0-2a91-50f3-ba29-21df42a1673f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20e15b4c-4991-5dc9-8b45-ed10cc092a7d" :answer/id #uuid "20e15b4c-4991-5dc9-8b45-ed10cc092a7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a53b2d0-48eb-5228-9ea2-2000e2948d59" :answer/id #uuid "4a53b2d0-48eb-5228-9ea2-2000e2948d59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aae46bc-e0c9-5b85-8008-b239c57b2c11" :answer/id #uuid "5aae46bc-e0c9-5b85-8008-b239c57b2c11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1ffc7c4-55dc-5d3f-b3b3-8c34240ac735" :answer/id #uuid "d1ffc7c4-55dc-5d3f-b3b3-8c34240ac735" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e56a686-12c8-5112-a821-3890332df74f" :question/id #uuid "7e56a686-12c8-5112-a821-3890332df74f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97423" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=3t-2{t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=3t-2{t}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(0)=0,\\,p(1)=1,\\,p(2)=-2.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(0)p(0)+p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(0)]^{2}+[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a799a47-68c0-5f48-82fe-456dcddb2abb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a799a47-68c0-5f48-82fe-456dcddb2abb" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a799a47-68c0-5f48-82fe-456dcddb2abb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1fa00b09-aa2e-5df4-ac96-221f4752071a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3425195d-9aff-555f-aedc-3c2ff2394fd5" "answer-5a6813eb-8d5c-5891-9a6f-a20bbd0d60b9" "answer-a269ca2f-0828-50e2-9c13-2e3788349d15" "answer-1fa00b09-aa2e-5df4-ac96-221f4752071a" "answer-116df7c4-ec26-5aa2-9b40-416a961b75bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3425195d-9aff-555f-aedc-3c2ff2394fd5" :answer/id #uuid "3425195d-9aff-555f-aedc-3c2ff2394fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a6813eb-8d5c-5891-9a6f-a20bbd0d60b9" :answer/id #uuid "5a6813eb-8d5c-5891-9a6f-a20bbd0d60b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a269ca2f-0828-50e2-9c13-2e3788349d15" :answer/id #uuid "a269ca2f-0828-50e2-9c13-2e3788349d15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{197}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fa00b09-aa2e-5df4-ac96-221f4752071a" :answer/id #uuid "1fa00b09-aa2e-5df4-ac96-221f4752071a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-116df7c4-ec26-5aa2-9b40-416a961b75bd" :answer/id #uuid "116df7c4-ec26-5aa2-9b40-416a961b75bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135035 :tutorial/content "Any vector space $V$ combined with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ on $V$ is called an **inner product space.** \n\nWhen referring to inner product spaces, we often say that the vector space $V$ is *equipped* with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$\n\nThe **norm** of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in V$ in an inner product space is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis definition allows us to apply the geometric concept of length to vectors defined in abstract vector spaces. This is because the norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ in an inner product space is a generalization of the norm (or length) of a vector in Euclidean space. To see this, recall that for $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert = \\sqrt{\\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}},\n$$\n\nand we have already seen that the dot product is itself an inner product.\n\nLet's consider an example. Suppose that an inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  3x_1 y_1 + 2x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the vector $\\mathbf v\\in V,$ given by\n![](images/46/46f92b1fb6f84950c98449deaab0c2574c10dfe01abe8c0c0d9258a57eee29ad.svg)\n\nComputing $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)+2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+2\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+8+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135036 :tutorial/content "Recall that a unit vector is a vector whose length is equal to $1.$ If we divide a non-zero vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb R^n$ by its norm $\\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert,$ we obtain a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\Vert\\mathbf{x}\\Vert}\n$$\n\nThe process of constructing a unit vector by dividing $\\mathbf x$ by its norm is called **normalization.** \n\nWe normalize vectors in more general inner product spaces using the same method. \n\nFor example, consider an inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle =  2x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + x_3 y_3.$  Let's normalize the following vector with respect to our inner product:  \n\n![](images/40/40bcf2af70449b87fe001d3864642c88437aecca18bc0750cff09643dc2edc00.svg) \n\nThe norm of a vector $\\mathbf v$ with respect to an inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =2\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)+3\\cdot (1\\cdot 1)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8+3+4 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $\\mathbf v$, we divide $\\mathbf v$ by its norm $\\|\\mathbf v\\| \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133985 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100848 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-856534cf-3669-5864-adb1-aa4947ae5494"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100669 :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below and the point $(0,9)$ lies on the curve. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0c3acc57c74ced95e7387e285d9de1c629e7724fe9e111b13df209198069e8d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y = f(x)$ passes through the point $(0, 9)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,7).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27de6227dee35cb8a7eaf2873409dfd6ad8c3c5f8d0cbd0de9ac223925b3fd76.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100887 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c103a5cd-d8c5-5183-99b4-f60981d83dcb" "question-f5dc650c-6521-56fb-b358-5191cc78caea"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100712 :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x) +2$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)?$\n\n![](images/f5/f5404d5fbf52098eaa7161f0219e4df98a3ae9f5b6b7b6b0220089d62abff82d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y = f(x) + 2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *up*. \n\nSo, the graph $y = f(x)$ would be $2$ units *below* the graph of $y = f(x) + 2.$\n\n![](images/0c/0ce67d91fd6c15d97e882c785392f001a4f38ed9bedd50d8631b34e4d5543adc.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100849 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3149eba2-f75e-505c-909c-9beed59b368d" "question-2b584399-1501-573d-8e9c-16d0bbc631a2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100670 :question/problem "The point $A$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$ and the point $B$ with coordinates $(2,-4)$ lies on the curve $y = g(x).$ If the curve $y = g(x)$ is related to $y = f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x) = f(x) + k$, then what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the point $B(2, -4)$ lies $3$ units *below* the point $A(2, -1).$ So, the graph of $y = g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units *down*. \n\n![](images/46/4656ae5ff75132fc9b760b6a8f73b0b434a8f66ecd621fa974a265df1d0d8a2c.png)\n\nAs a result, $k = -3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100847 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b9eb1708-044c-5408-bdf8-03d4b09b76eb" "question-e09e1da8-e383-596f-bdbd-80147bb9fb19"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100668 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)+3$ is obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units *up*. \n\nHence, if the point $(3,4)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, then the point \n\n$$\n(3,4+3) = (3,7)\n$$\n\nlies on $y=f(x) + 3.$\n\n![](images/57/577911187229d6a56a8885e4cea2e25752615ed7bb807768abf4b54c5073d177.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(3,4)$ lies on $y = f(x)$, we have that $f(3) = 4.$\n\nNow, if $x = 3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(3)+3 \\\\  & =4+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(3,7)$ must lie on $y = f(x) + 3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101206 :question/problem "![](images/82/82012efd1fbf78238ce1b6ecc49819283976db8f39ac5f57ec232a5eda8029dc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0,1)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(0,1)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)+2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *up*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,3).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f0a46e8f6ef1daac4acbaf49e5fb1ffc21046cbfc6260fe6dbe51d1814a1c12.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098571 :answer-field/correct 17592186098573 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-856534cf-3669-5864-adb1-aa4947ae5494" :question/id #uuid "856534cf-3669-5864-adb1-aa4947ae5494" :question/math-academy-id "q-108147" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e59a35140e008485fc092f716c318ff1ade67b68a8b9988150308af576759466.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0,3)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(0,3)$ as shown in the diagram. \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-1$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $1$ unit *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,2).$\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/56/56c48b2115d50b1d4e6ab105ba8214275a43efe0267857370976df55a205252a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d53ea49b-a4ff-5e2f-a0e2-8747562b72f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d53ea49b-a4ff-5e2f-a0e2-8747562b72f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "d53ea49b-a4ff-5e2f-a0e2-8747562b72f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2d992e4a-61ac-52f1-bc1b-70dc3650f4cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c10fae32-9c98-5d0c-ac63-9f70c6572aa6" "answer-536561c5-116e-5bc9-b117-97c9aa909d9f" "answer-c560d190-312c-5059-98d3-9dd7221f7fd9" "answer-2d992e4a-61ac-52f1-bc1b-70dc3650f4cb" "answer-8b85c6fd-b36c-5f3e-aa92-ef2e87392112"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c10fae32-9c98-5d0c-ac63-9f70c6572aa6" :answer/id #uuid "c10fae32-9c98-5d0c-ac63-9f70c6572aa6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/01/01e3f9c35d113349c65c2783bc23b3e43b