n "To graph the function $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed813305ba5087c0b70c6756c2a0b3c7c1957fad1a902e7b1c2540d4f727f3b6.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75b469e4-87d9-5644-95e1-b70f51e5abc3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75b469e4-87d9-5644-95e1-b70f51e5abc3" :answer-field/id #uuid "75b469e4-87d9-5644-95e1-b70f51e5abc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f84989d1-4804-511f-ba86-f03afe3b1757" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c141f488-8032-5f30-adb9-71d273cf9168" "answer-e20db687-e55a-51ad-9a51-af96cf0cfabd" "answer-da57a17f-a3ee-534b-9cf3-facfadf3eb92" "answer-f84989d1-4804-511f-ba86-f03afe3b1757" "answer-8639a17b-0e74-5099-9b06-91206ac6da9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c141f488-8032-5f30-adb9-71d273cf9168" :answer/id #uuid "c141f488-8032-5f30-adb9-71d273cf9168" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/41/41250ff7c9183c6b1786f62e5a8079b787d886cce07cdd5426b40eab6892c2a8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e20db687-e55a-51ad-9a51-af96cf0cfabd" :answer/id #uuid "e20db687-e55a-51ad-9a51-af96cf0cfabd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/94/94095ff759725727f042aae649691be7dbd7181b385f5d440867f926838981b9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da57a17f-a3ee-534b-9cf3-facfadf3eb92" :answer/id #uuid "da57a17f-a3ee-534b-9cf3-facfadf3eb92" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b3/b394d98d63e0ab17d6ac29ed9497a8b71b45d0884dc7beaa03aa28e34130d1db.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f84989d1-4804-511f-ba86-f03afe3b1757" :answer/id #uuid "f84989d1-4804-511f-ba86-f03afe3b1757" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/dd/dd5b4582e16d4eb13e72461f508e20d20e79b3fe4457b42013b058d25711f170.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8639a17b-0e74-5099-9b06-91206ac6da9e" :answer/id #uuid "8639a17b-0e74-5099-9b06-91206ac6da9e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e0/e05a4b4686162c4e36f02f368ac501dc3195280d4b47389e529e14fc0e028734.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dfd0113c-14f1-5d6a-ac3b-dca77d3d998d" :question/id #uuid "dfd0113c-14f1-5d6a-ac3b-dca77d3d998d" :question/math-academy-id "q-26451" :question/problem "![](images/66/66d9e6755720befa3887a3c0e65833f396ce7442ae04f00c3d82721cfc804947.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "To graph the function $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cfa455809238b0d89c1b496283f6a8be602fa481e98a5e1d8ac72c48346f3703.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9b540ace-3a60-55e4-9b9e-91feba3a81a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b540ace-3a60-55e4-9b9e-91feba3a81a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b540ace-3a60-55e4-9b9e-91feba3a81a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f4385e57-3e44-5483-b080-70fb5b3d4295" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d19bf650-bc2a-58df-8b1a-1c95441ca1db" "answer-3d818015-0b2b-5b0f-9dd0-19d7a8414e29" "answer-11404e03-5065-5a00-9c70-e6cf6344f30d" "answer-f4385e57-3e44-5483-b080-70fb5b3d4295" "answer-034d60dd-9303-51e6-b82e-a264e93f31db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d19bf650-bc2a-58df-8b1a-1c95441ca1db" :answer/id #uuid "d19bf650-bc2a-58df-8b1a-1c95441ca1db" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/0e/0ef41074fd8912a624a22366239b1a74d66c8f922b2dc113a120441080b51e36.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d818015-0b2b-5b0f-9dd0-19d7a8414e29" :answer/id #uuid "3d818015-0b2b-5b0f-9dd0-19d7a8414e29" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/67/673b42150092582128c0d347693e8e6edc131b79de1c269bc75693a529a59687.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11404e03-5065-5a00-9c70-e6cf6344f30d" :answer/id #uuid "11404e03-5065-5a00-9c70-e6cf6344f30d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/cb/cbd4ac741abc77c6bef177d10e4b0289c5af9cca6cb63a5131f67d0563e4c33d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4385e57-3e44-5483-b080-70fb5b3d4295" :answer/id #uuid "f4385e57-3e44-5483-b080-70fb5b3d4295" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d2/d24424c2298a487a5a3a4225fb0a77c2f8d2a254fa255182445ab5b0227f9b0c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-034d60dd-9303-51e6-b82e-a264e93f31db" :answer/id #uuid "034d60dd-9303-51e6-b82e-a264e93f31db" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/db/dbd98697b2f9e2586972258e9d7514dcd70201c9968878226c0c5968edf78e87.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2708eacc-b12f-5049-bb93-6886b57484fd" :question/id #uuid "2708eacc-b12f-5049-bb93-6886b57484fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-111185" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,6)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,6)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(3,\\frac{6}{3})=(3,2),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c861d536-4b73-5bd5-a586-cd534a3b924d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c861d536-4b73-5bd5-a586-cd534a3b924d" :answer-field/id #uuid "c861d536-4b73-5bd5-a586-cd534a3b924d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0de34e6e-ce64-5552-8a5c-f69eb0490ff9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f50c2d83-0310-5633-bc1e-2fbf9b92c4d1" "answer-e0d3dda4-f2bf-5297-875e-8af145212de6" "answer-2af0d1ed-dd1d-56ae-8b44-42cea59b8320" "answer-0de34e6e-ce64-5552-8a5c-f69eb0490ff9" "answer-922c8552-c8fc-5577-b29a-57d3afc8d382"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f50c2d83-0310-5633-bc1e-2fbf9b92c4d1" :answer/id #uuid "f50c2d83-0310-5633-bc1e-2fbf9b92c4d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0d3dda4-f2bf-5297-875e-8af145212de6" :answer/id #uuid "e0d3dda4-f2bf-5297-875e-8af145212de6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2af0d1ed-dd1d-56ae-8b44-42cea59b8320" :answer/id #uuid "2af0d1ed-dd1d-56ae-8b44-42cea59b8320" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0de34e6e-ce64-5552-8a5c-f69eb0490ff9" :answer/id #uuid "0de34e6e-ce64-5552-8a5c-f69eb0490ff9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-922c8552-c8fc-5577-b29a-57d3afc8d382" :answer/id #uuid "922c8552-c8fc-5577-b29a-57d3afc8d382" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1e8554d-3b9d-57c5-a60f-771660835934" :question/id #uuid "e1e8554d-3b9d-57c5-a60f-771660835934" :question/math-academy-id "q-81962" :question/problem "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,-3)$ lies on the curve $y=3f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=3f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe point $Q(3,q)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $P(3,-3)$ on $y=3f(x).$ Since we know that the $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $3$ times the $y$-coordinate of $Q,$ we can form the following equation:\n\n$$\n3q=-3\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get $q=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(3,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2f02c8f-0f0c-5292-993d-c05cc073b492"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2f02c8f-0f0c-5292-993d-c05cc073b492" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f02c8f-0f0c-5292-993d-c05cc073b492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c44dcde3-6a08-5c35-97da-32bf50958d8f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-00281f2f-3751-58c9-b3d0-f40bdf637ad3" "answer-8d05d53d-f155-50da-8d25-4a7f53e9e530" "answer-c44dcde3-6a08-5c35-97da-32bf50958d8f" "answer-c54d08b4-7eae-5ffb-bfa7-63547d34abe9" "answer-46a3b3f1-6794-587b-8780-372316f14734"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00281f2f-3751-58c9-b3d0-f40bdf637ad3" :answer/id #uuid "00281f2f-3751-58c9-b3d0-f40bdf637ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d05d53d-f155-50da-8d25-4a7f53e9e530" :answer/id #uuid "8d05d53d-f155-50da-8d25-4a7f53e9e530" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c44dcde3-6a08-5c35-97da-32bf50958d8f" :answer/id #uuid "c44dcde3-6a08-5c35-97da-32bf50958d8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c54d08b4-7eae-5ffb-bfa7-63547d34abe9" :answer/id #uuid "c54d08b4-7eae-5ffb-bfa7-63547d34abe9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,-9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46a3b3f1-6794-587b-8780-372316f14734" :answer/id #uuid "46a3b3f1-6794-587b-8780-372316f14734" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(9,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101970 :tutorial/content "When we multiply a function by a constant, the graph either stretches out or compresses. Transforming a function in this way is called **scaling**.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21f9b06e470ca0e94605eb72c5a12d2a1f2ec56645588a960f12be81ad8252ec.png)\n\nThe following table lists four sets of points. The set of points in the left-hand column correspond to the graph of $y=f(x),$ and the set on the right-hand side corresponds to $y=2f(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=2f(x)[/math]} \\\\ (1,2) & (1,4) \\\\ (4,-3) & (4,-6) \\\\ (0,2) & (0,4) &  \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x),$ there is a point on the graph of $y=2f(x)$ with the same $x$-value and *double* the $y$-value. \n\nNow that we have four points on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ let's plot the full graph.\n\n![](images/26/262b6515b995f6fdcf7b959dc62d9599b71476471fbc2bde394dcebfd47cabc0.png)\n\nWe say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **vertically stretched** by a **scale factor** of $2.$ \n\nWe can also say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ stretched by a factor of $2$ **parallel to the $\\mathbf y$-axis.**" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101092 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133868 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ec696af-4927-5944-9a02-9b2829e4a66e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133684 :question/problem "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that ![](images/7c/7c21bcd5cfd8a91b756c7d1f62b75099a7dc122b3d0460f63b860f2a5804063a.svg) and ![](images/3a/3a1cf39c7f0e8f3200bbd54ca35b67ec54bebff2d88c464ba31eaad045fbaa0f.svg) are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133867 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ab0bc45b-46cc-5674-97dd-e2aa819a42e4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133869 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b0a4298c-3fe3-56e6-a8b6-d32ef03d865a" "question-63be8186-8026-5f56-8153-6a18a81d6ce3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133685 :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(t) g(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t)=\\sin 2t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = 0$\n\nII. $\\langle f(t), g(t) \\rangle = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$\n\nIII. $f(t) \\perp g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity* $\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta=\\dfrac12(\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ec696af-4927-5944-9a02-9b2829e4a66e" :question/id #uuid "3ec696af-4927-5944-9a02-9b2829e4a66e" :question/math-academy-id "q-44092" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (3\\cdot (-4))+(-1)\\cdot k \\\\  & =-4-48-k \\\\  & =-52-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -52-k & =0 \\\\ k & =-52\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f73f8354-0a92-5c1b-b79c-ddccc0b399bf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f73f8354-0a92-5c1b-b79c-ddccc0b399bf" :answer-field/id #uuid "f73f8354-0a92-5c1b-b79c-ddccc0b399bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4f689529-3570-51cb-8fa4-878e75544ffd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2332eb35-32b1-5de9-a2e9-1ddf26943cf3" "answer-4f689529-3570-51cb-8fa4-878e75544ffd" "answer-532ace63-b6c8-5ac3-b8c5-4aa2d837f01f" "answer-a63909ae-37e1-5328-b066-e8543eae494b" "answer-7ee3b41a-75cf-5b0d-8a12-1b636f5397f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2332eb35-32b1-5de9-a2e9-1ddf26943cf3" :answer/id #uuid "2332eb35-32b1-5de9-a2e9-1ddf26943cf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f689529-3570-51cb-8fa4-878e75544ffd" :answer/id #uuid "4f689529-3570-51cb-8fa4-878e75544ffd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-532ace63-b6c8-5ac3-b8c5-4aa2d837f01f" :answer/id #uuid "532ace63-b6c8-5ac3-b8c5-4aa2d837f01f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-56" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a63909ae-37e1-5328-b066-e8543eae494b" :answer/id #uuid "a63909ae-37e1-5328-b066-e8543eae494b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ee3b41a-75cf-5b0d-8a12-1b636f5397f6" :answer/id #uuid "7ee3b41a-75cf-5b0d-8a12-1b636f5397f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ab0bc45b-46cc-5674-97dd-e2aa819a42e4" :question/id #uuid "ab0bc45b-46cc-5674-97dd-e2aa819a42e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-140268" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=k$ and $y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }k(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+{k}^{2}t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{k}^{2}\\pi -k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}\\pi  & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e906b0d7-9bfe-5f5a-8ec1-525aba8cd263"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e906b0d7-9bfe-5f5a-8ec1-525aba8cd263" :answer-field/id #uuid "e906b0d7-9bfe-5f5a-8ec1-525aba8cd263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd418d0c-3e25-5a0e-ba95-c6fccb312156" :answer-field/choices ["answer-567ce31d-989b-5c7e-9a87-8eb4eda3a20b" "answer-8066afe7-552a-5367-a39e-ca5263ea4ed8" "answer-fd418d0c-3e25-5a0e-ba95-c6fccb312156" "answer-e8c728c0-f21b-5028-a87c-01f7f35d9f4b" "answer-ed57322e-f8b7-5fdc-96a0-76753e11e9b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-567ce31d-989b-5c7e-9a87-8eb4eda3a20b" :answer/id #uuid "567ce31d-989b-5c7e-9a87-8eb4eda3a20b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8066afe7-552a-5367-a39e-ca5263ea4ed8" :answer/id #uuid "8066afe7-552a-5367-a39e-ca5263ea4ed8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd418d0c-3e25-5a0e-ba95-c6fccb312156" :answer/id #uuid "fd418d0c-3e25-5a0e-ba95-c6fccb312156" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8c728c0-f21b-5028-a87c-01f7f35d9f4b" :answer/id #uuid "e8c728c0-f21b-5028-a87c-01f7f35d9f4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed57322e-f8b7-5fdc-96a0-76753e11e9b2" :answer/id #uuid "ed57322e-f8b7-5fdc-96a0-76753e11e9b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134556 :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)=k-5{t}^{3}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t(k-5{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(kt-5{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}-{t}^{5}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2k-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2k-32 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =32 \\\\ k & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133374 :answer-field/correct 17592186133376 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0a4298c-3fe3-56e6-a8b6-d32ef03d865a" :question/id #uuid "b0a4298c-3fe3-56e6-a8b6-d32ef03d865a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140437" :question/problem "Let $V=C[1,e]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[1,e],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=2$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2e-2$\n\nII. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2$\n\nIII. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ {v}^{′}=2 & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t){|}_{1}^{e}-{\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2e-0)-2t{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2e-(2e-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d138d1f-8404-5c7e-9be4-28c7d32e629d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d138d1f-8404-5c7e-9be4-28c7d32e629d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d138d1f-8404-5c7e-9be4-28c7d32e629d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-540d63e9-f5c1-5de3-aa75-5e54a26c3d9d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50dfb39b-a141-5a07-9dc1-e26173048d6b" "answer-f8cb8768-2e19-5690-8039-5c36da1bd9ae" "answer-d89a593c-5cbd-5bd4-b9d4-6b60073d294b" "answer-540d63e9-f5c1-5de3-aa75-5e54a26c3d9d" "answer-aac28d6e-780f-508f-aa9d-1af1c8430710"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50dfb39b-a141-5a07-9dc1-e26173048d6b" :answer/id #uuid "50dfb39b-a141-5a07-9dc1-e26173048d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8cb8768-2e19-5690-8039-5c36da1bd9ae" :answer/id #uuid "f8cb8768-2e19-5690-8039-5c36da1bd9ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d89a593c-5cbd-5bd4-b9d4-6b60073d294b" :answer/id #uuid "d89a593c-5cbd-5bd4-b9d4-6b60073d294b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-540d63e9-f5c1-5de3-aa75-5e54a26c3d9d" :answer/id #uuid "540d63e9-f5c1-5de3-aa75-5e54a26c3d9d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aac28d6e-780f-508f-aa9d-1af1c8430710" :answer/id #uuid "aac28d6e-780f-508f-aa9d-1af1c8430710" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-63be8186-8026-5f56-8153-6a18a81d6ce3" :question/id #uuid "63be8186-8026-5f56-8153-6a18a81d6ce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-44066" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n\nII. $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n\nIII. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}{3}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )}{3}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2a7c0e4-497b-555e-b210-485ed2c4815e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2a7c0e4-497b-555e-b210-485ed2c4815e" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a7c0e4-497b-555e-b210-485ed2c4815e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4dd94adf-0738-5e8a-8201-c8eaf6da5741" :answer-field/choices ["answer-688f977f-48f9-5555-8bb1-1d938d96b3a5" "answer-bd83ca4f-fc8b-5c42-9c89-2ce30742bfbd" "answer-4dd94adf-0738-5e8a-8201-c8eaf6da5741" "answer-354f5fb0-502e-5b97-96b1-1770af96065f" "answer-390a92f2-20da-56ad-8a6d-81f1d663abf6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-688f977f-48f9-5555-8bb1-1d938d96b3a5" :answer/id #uuid "688f977f-48f9-5555-8bb1-1d938d96b3a5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd83ca4f-fc8b-5c42-9c89-2ce30742bfbd" :answer/id #uuid "bd83ca4f-fc8b-5c42-9c89-2ce30742bfbd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dd94adf-0738-5e8a-8201-c8eaf6da5741" :answer/id #uuid "4dd94adf-0738-5e8a-8201-c8eaf6da5741" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-354f5fb0-502e-5b97-96b1-1770af96065f" :answer/id #uuid "354f5fb0-502e-5b97-96b1-1770af96065f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-390a92f2-20da-56ad-8a6d-81f1d663abf6" :answer/id #uuid "390a92f2-20da-56ad-8a6d-81f1d663abf6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135101 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ be vectors in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle.$ \n\nTwo vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ are **orthogonal with respect to the inner product** $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V$ if their inner product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nIf the two vectors are orthogonal, then we denote this by $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}.$ For instance, the vectors \n\n![](images/3d/3d118ca8f8b3ea3e1b978d7bcd0159b1774ea4e8848f39d3a2566e6931b4c2f2.svg)\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x} , \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1y_1 + 2x_2y_2 + x_3y_3\n$$\n\nare orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (1\\cdot (-1))+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2-2+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ in a vector space $V$ equipped with an inner product $\\langle \\cdot , \\cdot \\rangle$ is orthogonal to any vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in V$ since $\\langle \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{x} \\rangle = 0.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133987 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3689d124-7069-5ac1-b495-7dcfa807134a" "question-5bab4cd0-ae02-5029-8d61-df09bcee19e2" "question-17f34adc-72ed-5271-a021-3a7f1d773e6b" "question-6b5fc782-a821-5ef2-823c-11a0d288b2b3" "question-2633d33a-0706-5b9b-a985-6a0050cda4a1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203875 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4 \\times 2$ matrix\n![](images/23/232f6bffd41437d85b7a0f09ec268d459009656ef77fba4b4fb445b52dd13a1d.svg)\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.689 & -0.424 & 0.495 & 0.318 \\\\ 0.143 & -0.241 & -0.524 & 0.804 \\\\ 0.695 & -0.188 & 0.650 & 0.244 \\\\ -0.149 & 0.853 & 0.241 & 0.439\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.075 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.294 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.709 & -0.706 \\\\ 0.706 & -0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance." :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $X^TX.$ Using the SVD $X = U\\Sigma V^T$ and the orthogonality of $U,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\Sigma^T\\Sigma$ is diagonal with the squared singular values on the diagonal, the eigenvectors of $X^TX$ are the columns of $V,$ and the eigenvalues are the squared singular values. The largest eigenvalue corresponds to the first singular value $\\sigma_1 = 2.075,$ so the first principal component is $v_1,$ the first column of $V{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e91c75818de82c4564584df51a72432ebc97c90dd5a1c8687b24bd31998d9910.svg)\n\nTo obtain the one-dimensional version of the data, we project $X$ onto $v_1$ by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1.40 & -0.62 \\\\ 0.01 & 0.43 \\\\ -0.85 & 1.19 \\\\ -0.56 & -1.00\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.709 \\\\ 0.706\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.43 \\\\ 0.30 \\\\ 1.44 \\\\ -0.31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can compute this projection using $u_1 \\sigma_1,$ where $u_1$ is the first column of $U.$ Indeed, from the SVD,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X{v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}{v}_{1}=UΣ[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}{\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u_1$ and $\\sigma_1 = 2.075,$ we get\n\n![](images/14/148bc9bd4289245d9ada00da68f7b6e0666bfa9c3fffd47fde79164b177732d8.svg)\n\nwhich confirms the result.\n\n**Watch out!** The vector $v_1$ itself is the first principal component *direction* — a unit vector in feature space. It is not a one-dimensional representation of the data. The one-dimensional representation is $Xv_1$ (or equivalently $u_1\\sigma_1$), which is a $4 \\times 1$ vector containing the projection of each data point onto this direction." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9ccff77b-7d01-5bd7-aaf4-9fba844066ae" "question-3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" "question-439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" "question-c6804c35-f8e1-5715-b170-24ee05ad3186"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203876 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n![](images/83/83365da559075cf32eaebf67a12eef548f40585a84a3226aad488327de8e9e84.svg)\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.177 & 0.014 & 0.637 & -0.004 \\\\ 0.275 & -0.054 & -0.847 & 0.36 & 0.274 \\\\ -0.213 & -0.653 & 0.171 & -0.068 & 0.703 \\\\ 0.542 & -0.181 & 0.459 & 0.678 & -0.05 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.711 & 0.206 & -0.022 & 0.655\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.272 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.031 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.659 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.724 & 0.019 & 0.69 \\\\ 0.422 & 0.779 & -0.464 \\\\ 0.546 & -0.627 & -0.555\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nFill in the blanks below:\n\nWe should pick components based on \n![](images/21/21182b5f0667421011af540ed046b0924b738d74d6a09f574320bd4768df7c38.svg)\n\nIf we wish to preserve $75\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n![](images/ab/abbb30d852a431e139075cbe6686ea1aafd697e5d52351e1f23730545ea2d661.svg)." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = 2.272, \\qquad\n\\sigma_2 = 2.031, \\qquad\n\\sigma_3 = 1.659.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} =\n\\dfrac{2.272^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 42.9\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sigma_i^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} =\n\\dfrac{2.272^2 + 2.031^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 77.1\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $42.9\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $77.1\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/f3/f35a54aa598a0c65fc14ae807b60ddd6dcb68a36dd4f0ba5089fae12ec7a9495.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to ![](images/8d/8d0e762e494aa7a29af18cae486e8fca07aa9e6fb1c68e1204b8e1765caee8c2.svg) since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $75\\%$ of the variance." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d455f69c-b6d0-5a22-baea-4655fa27f3c7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203877 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n\n![](images/c2/c2163de699b69fa804ad71bf683badb2e6e76115d88b444606f39e46eaa7aed9.svg)\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nLet's walk through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset." :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the direction in feature space}}$ with the $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{most}}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{orthogonal}}$ to the first $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{principal component}}$ with the most variance.\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{standardize the data}}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n![](images/15/159be3f32150b2a56f4a335990cef0c71cf6362447fc4aff521751743ab2ef58.svg)\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.688 & 0.370 & -0.421 & 0.197 & 0.417 \\\\ 0.160 & 0.258 & 0.226 & 0.910 & -0.168 \\\\ 0.642 & 0.396 & -0.101 & -0.081 & 0.644 \\\\ 0.147 & -0.753 & -0.451 & 0.353 & 0.289 \\\\ -0.260 & -0.271 & 0.747 & 0.038 & 0.547\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.825 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.939 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.543 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.607 & 0.503 & -0.615 \\\\ -0.383 & 0.864 & 0.327 \\\\ -0.696 & -0.037 & -0.717\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2.825^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{66.3\\%}.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2.825^2 + 1.939^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{97.6\\%}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $66.3\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $97.6\\%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{2}$ dimensions, since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting $v_1, v_2, v_3$ for the columns of $V,$ the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\nX_{\\text{red}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{X_{\\text{std}} \\cdot [v_1, v_2]},\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3689d124-7069-5ac1-b495-7dcfa807134a" :question/id #uuid "3689d124-7069-5ac1-b495-7dcfa807134a" :question/math-academy-id "q-355838" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 & 0.813 & 0.505 & 0.021 \\\\ -0.469 & -0.212 & 0.634 & -0.578 \\\\ 0.676 & -0.54 & 0.473 & 0.168 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.061 & 0.345 & 0.798\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.065 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.316 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & -0.705 \\\\ 0.705 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ -0.469 \\\\ 0.676 \\\\ -0.49\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.065$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a0babfda-6373-531b-92eb-9560a146bc71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a0babfda-6373-531b-92eb-9560a146bc71" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0babfda-6373-531b-92eb-9560a146bc71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a843bc1-b154-5a46-a21d-415e36ea992a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8eed707b-6371-5999-839b-4f5cd51042db" "answer-93bad0d2-4d8d-5501-8d17-ee62bd3e4da8" "answer-9a843bc1-b154-5a46-a21d-415e36ea992a" "answer-d27dd8c5-0a47-555c-b549-d93700db5c64" "answer-3f38a1d5-5e26-5720-933a-074463f63291"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8eed707b-6371-5999-839b-4f5cd51042db" :answer/id #uuid "8eed707b-6371-5999-839b-4f5cd51042db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93bad0d2-4d8d-5501-8d17-ee62bd3e4da8" :answer/id #uuid "93bad0d2-4d8d-5501-8d17-ee62bd3e4da8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a843bc1-b154-5a46-a21d-415e36ea992a" :answer/id #uuid "9a843bc1-b154-5a46-a21d-415e36ea992a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d27dd8c5-0a47-555c-b549-d93700db5c64" :answer/id #uuid "d27dd8c5-0a47-555c-b549-d93700db5c64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f38a1d5-5e26-5720-933a-074463f63291" :answer/id #uuid "3f38a1d5-5e26-5720-933a-074463f63291" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5bab4cd0-ae02-5029-8d61-df09bcee19e2" :question/id #uuid "5bab4cd0-ae02-5029-8d61-df09bcee19e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-355832" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.191 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.095 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0408fb21-e6d7-5afd-8fc7-4e0712c6e323"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0408fb21-e6d7-5afd-8fc7-4e0712c6e323" :answer-field/id #uuid "0408fb21-e6d7-5afd-8fc7-4e0712c6e323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ff685870-d8f7-56d3-a9cf-f7dffe500056" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ebbbe6b-7c84-5932-a277-a4405916dccd" "answer-124dd69d-b9ac-532a-85d8-fd7b70eecf8d" "answer-bddffe44-aacb-5f0d-a38d-a2f7fc732660" "answer-ff685870-d8f7-56d3-a9cf-f7dffe500056" "answer-36ca5ef3-24a6-5fb1-8622-0b1d7a9443b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ebbbe6b-7c84-5932-a277-a4405916dccd" :answer/id #uuid "3ebbbe6b-7c84-5932-a277-a4405916dccd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-124dd69d-b9ac-532a-85d8-fd7b70eecf8d" :answer/id #uuid "124dd69d-b9ac-532a-85d8-fd7b70eecf8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bddffe44-aacb-5f0d-a38d-a2f7fc732660" :answer/id #uuid "bddffe44-aacb-5f0d-a38d-a2f7fc732660" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff685870-d8f7-56d3-a9cf-f7dffe500056" :answer/id #uuid "ff685870-d8f7-56d3-a9cf-f7dffe500056" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36ca5ef3-24a6-5fb1-8622-0b1d7a9443b3" :answer/id #uuid "36ca5ef3-24a6-5fb1-8622-0b1d7a9443b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-17f34adc-72ed-5271-a021-3a7f1d773e6b" :question/id #uuid "17f34adc-72ed-5271-a021-3a7f1d773e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-355834" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.56 & -0.17 & 0.71 & -0.393 \\\\ 0.682 & -0.53 & 0.486 & 0.135 \\\\ 0.264 & 0.821 & 0.485 & 0.144 \\\\ -0.39 & -0.126 & 0.159 & 0.898\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.085 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.292 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & 0.708 \\\\ 0.708 & -0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-07619765-e1d4-5971-ad43-4b34eeca2055"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-07619765-e1d4-5971-ad43-4b34eeca2055" :answer-field/id #uuid "07619765-e1d4-5971-ad43-4b34eeca2055" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4c09282e-d0f3-5c75-9b06-861585a630c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-968f6982-b7e0-5819-8694-ea0749ae1d73" "answer-e36fb8c0-2d56-5128-88b0-bc68d95661d4" "answer-36559b24-53e6-5745-8a45-9fd571a78363" "answer-158c5e57-6484-540e-8dae-1f773f268a50" "answer-4c09282e-d0f3-5c75-9b06-861585a630c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-968f6982-b7e0-5819-8694-ea0749ae1d73" :answer/id #uuid "968f6982-b7e0-5819-8694-ea0749ae1d73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e36fb8c0-2d56-5128-88b0-bc68d95661d4" :answer/id #uuid "e36fb8c0-2d56-5128-88b0-bc68d95661d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36559b24-53e6-5745-8a45-9fd571a78363" :answer/id #uuid "36559b24-53e6-5745-8a45-9fd571a78363" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-158c5e57-6484-540e-8dae-1f773f268a50" :answer/id #uuid "158c5e57-6484-540e-8dae-1f773f268a50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c09282e-d0f3-5c75-9b06-861585a630c3" :answer/id #uuid "4c09282e-d0f3-5c75-9b06-861585a630c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b5fc782-a821-5ef2-823c-11a0d288b2b3" :question/id #uuid "6b5fc782-a821-5ef2-823c-11a0d288b2b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-355836" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.49 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 \\\\ 0.49 \\\\ 0.506 \\\\ -0.405\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 1.917$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48e3fb47-9a79-5df7-a139-217361ff8523"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48e3fb47-9a79-5df7-a139-217361ff8523" :answer-field/id #uuid "48e3fb47-9a79-5df7-a139-217361ff8523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14ba44ce-54ad-50d5-b6be-6a4ff5b285b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a77b766c-7b5b-551f-8c98-f81dfcdda2c3" "answer-14ba44ce-54ad-50d5-b6be-6a4ff5b285b9" "answer-a348c40f-1571-51e0-9281-80844369699e" "answer-117e5dbc-80f8-532a-8d97-3cd9d790be5c" "answer-d27bd917-8a17-55cb-9e24-d1df3af18407"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a77b766c-7b5b-551f-8c98-f81dfcdda2c3" :answer/id #uuid "a77b766c-7b5b-551f-8c98-f81dfcdda2c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14ba44ce-54ad-50d5-b6be-6a4ff5b285b9" :answer/id #uuid "14ba44ce-54ad-50d5-b6be-6a4ff5b285b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a348c40f-1571-51e0-9281-80844369699e" :answer/id #uuid "a348c40f-1571-51e0-9281-80844369699e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-117e5dbc-80f8-532a-8d97-3cd9d790be5c" :answer/id #uuid "117e5dbc-80f8-532a-8d97-3cd9d790be5c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d27bd917-8a17-55cb-9e24-d1df3af18407" :answer/id #uuid "d27bd917-8a17-55cb-9e24-d1df3af18407" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2633d33a-0706-5b9b-a985-6a0050cda4a1" :question/id #uuid "2633d33a-0706-5b9b-a985-6a0050cda4a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-355831" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.27 & 0.39 \\\\ -0.99 & -1.23 \\\\ -0.13 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.39 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.035 & 0.86 & 0.132 & 0.491 \\\\ -0.657 & -0.314 & -0.208 & 0.653 \\\\ -0.121 & -0.196 & 0.969 & 0.092 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.351 & -0.032 & 0.569\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.389 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.542 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ -0.657 \\\\ -0.121 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ 0.86 \\\\ 0.132 \\\\ 0.491\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III uses the values of the first row of $U$ multiplied by ${\\sigma }_{1}.$ However, the projection corresponds to the first column of $U.$ Thus, using the row vector is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87da0997-109c-5d59-8556-8db99822494e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87da0997-109c-5d59-8556-8db99822494e" :answer-field/id #uuid "87da0997-109c-5d59-8556-8db99822494e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee184618-27b0-5c25-9140-55a9d8379731" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8fc0fdcf-4cb8-5018-a940-071de4027293" "answer-ee184618-27b0-5c25-9140-55a9d8379731" "answer-1a907ca2-c523-5180-bcdc-bbe6f6c4642e" "answer-9d42bdf7-d6e9-55e1-86ce-2e629a5f8fc0" "answer-f805cefc-a8ea-5ac2-a5c1-3ba59d32a17f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fc0fdcf-4cb8-5018-a940-071de4027293" :answer/id #uuid "8fc0fdcf-4cb8-5018-a940-071de4027293" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee184618-27b0-5c25-9140-55a9d8379731" :answer/id #uuid "ee184618-27b0-5c25-9140-55a9d8379731" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a907ca2-c523-5180-bcdc-bbe6f6c4642e" :answer/id #uuid "1a907ca2-c523-5180-bcdc-bbe6f6c4642e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d42bdf7-d6e9-55e1-86ce-2e629a5f8fc0" :answer/id #uuid "9d42bdf7-d6e9-55e1-86ce-2e629a5f8fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f805cefc-a8ea-5ac2-a5c1-3ba59d32a17f" :answer/id #uuid "f805cefc-a8ea-5ac2-a5c1-3ba59d32a17f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ccff77b-7d01-5bd7-aaf4-9fba844066ae" :question/id #uuid "9ccff77b-7d01-5bd7-aaf4-9fba844066ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-355903" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.46 & -0.2 & 0.07 & -0.88 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.15 & 0.73 & -0.87 \\\\ -0.24 & 1.53 & 0.68 & 1.35 \\\\ -0.15 & -1.25 & 0.23 & 0.73 \\\\ 1.32 & -0.23 & -1.72 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.186 & 0.257 & 0.252 & -0.792 & -0.456 \\\\ 0.398 & 0.593 & 0.3 & 0.45 & -0.443 \\\\ 0.529 & -0.708 & 0.123 & -0.057 & -0.447 \\\\ -0.015 & 0.115 & -0.887 & 0.015 & -0.446 \\\\ -0.725 & -0.259 & 0.208 & 0.408 & -0.443\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.878 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.152 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.636 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.652 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & -0.419 & 0.045 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.38 & -0.525 & 0.761 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.654 & 0.205 & -0.148 & -0.713 \\\\ 0.262 & -0.712 & -0.63 & 0.166\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.878,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.152,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.636,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.652.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈51.7%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈80.6%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈97.3%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $51.7%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $80.6%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $97.3%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc57a7e978a8b123dd21da151c2b0fec3080be9d39f1c8f26cc3b19c0cbd2b1.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d87761c7-3333-5f96-b1b1-7878fa8dd615" "answer-field-bf9d5ef7-04cb-5f23-a601-17a6256765aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d87761c7-3333-5f96-b1b1-7878fa8dd615" :answer-field/id #uuid "d87761c7-3333-5f96-b1b1-7878fa8dd615" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-270f63c7-1aeb-558c-8a65-03c3f71ad498" "answer-3678144c-c5c5-59eb-9c73-7f7cfc99f4a6" "answer-8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" "answer-bb438be0-e790-540f-8d81-6dbdb5c89782"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-270f63c7-1aeb-558c-8a65-03c3f71ad498" :answer/id #uuid "270f63c7-1aeb-558c-8a65-03c3f71ad498" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3678144c-c5c5-59eb-9c73-7f7cfc99f4a6" :answer/id #uuid "3678144c-c5c5-59eb-9c73-7f7cfc99f4a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" :answer/id #uuid "8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb438be0-e790-540f-8d81-6dbdb5c89782" :answer/id #uuid "bb438be0-e790-540f-8d81-6dbdb5c89782" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf9d5ef7-04cb-5f23-a601-17a6256765aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9d5ef7-04cb-5f23-a601-17a6256765aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d6ed0a8-2992-50b5-a253-5ba14a0a4402" "answer-2fc905bf-a333-5f88-bc94-7532cf83b587" "answer-c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" "answer-9fe3ce25-405b-53e4-8190-780d13dc0791"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d6ed0a8-2992-50b5-a253-5ba14a0a4402" :answer/id #uuid "7d6ed0a8-2992-50b5-a253-5ba14a0a4402" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fc905bf-a333-5f88-bc94-7532cf83b587" :answer/id #uuid "2fc905bf-a333-5f88-bc94-7532cf83b587" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" :answer/id #uuid "c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fe3ce25-405b-53e4-8190-780d13dc0791" :answer/id #uuid "9fe3ce25-405b-53e4-8190-780d13dc0791" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204612 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.11 & -1.51 & -0.99 & -1.19 \\\\ -0.58 & 1.16 & 1.49 & 0.35 \\\\ -0.29 & 0.03 & 0.04 & -0.68 \\\\ 1.43 & 0.57 & -0.84 & 1.42 \\\\ 0.55 & -0.25 & 0.3 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.761 & 0.185 & 0.263 & 0.342 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.309 & -0.738 & 0.28 & 0.285 & 0.447 \\\\ -0.169 & -0.143 & -0.022 & -0.866 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.538 & 0.63 & 0.338 & 0.013 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.084 & 0.067 & -0.859 & 0.225 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.125 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.362 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.699 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.455 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.49 & 0.509 & -0.625 & -0.333 \\\\ 0.572 & -0.337 & 0.479 & -0.573 \\\\ 0.25 & -0.761 & -0.55 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.22 & 0.278 & 0.71\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.125,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.362,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.699,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.455.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.125}^{2}}{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}+{0.699}^{2}+{0.455}^{2}}≈60.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}}{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}+{0.699}^{2}+{0.455}^{2}}≈95.7%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $60.9%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $95.7%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/29/299ec97726335bb0dc02c7d256c7722eb52a7c22cd8b46a856d1b669bbe918dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0935b382-4574-59fb-a9b6-ea0fdb98be37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186203561 :answer-field/correct 17592186203562 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0935b382-4574-59fb-a9b6-ea0fdb98be37" :answer-field/id #uuid "0935b382-4574-59fb-a9b6-ea0fdb98be37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-cbd67190-901e-54cf-963f-8fa2f4f87575" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63ef7549-c7c2-58ca-92bb-1912806cb967" "answer-cbd67190-901e-54cf-963f-8fa2f4f87575" "answer-29e228fd-1f0b-50d7-9082-a4911c109df9" "answer-38b85c46-47af-5d2f-a564-e9efc73d7eec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63ef7549-c7c2-58ca-92bb-1912806cb967" :answer/id #uuid "63ef7549-c7c2-58ca-92bb-1912806cb967" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbd67190-901e-54cf-963f-8fa2f4f87575" :answer/id #uuid "cbd67190-901e-54cf-963f-8fa2f4f87575" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29e228fd-1f0b-50d7-9082-a4911c109df9" :answer/id #uuid "29e228fd-1f0b-50d7-9082-a4911c109df9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38b85c46-47af-5d2f-a564-e9efc73d7eec" :answer/id #uuid "38b85c46-47af-5d2f-a564-e9efc73d7eec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" :question/id #uuid "3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" :question/math-academy-id "q-355844" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.74 & 0.61 & 0.89 \\\\ 0.38 & 0 & 0.61 \\\\ -0.92 & -0.03 & -0.87 \\\\ -1.2 & -1.61 & -1.28 \\\\ 1.01 & 1.02 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.389 & 0.167 & -0.312 & 0.84 & -0.133 \\\\ -0.174 & 0.453 & -0.384 & -0.192 & 0.762 \\\\ 0.321 & -0.749 & -0.348 & 0.232 & 0.402 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.384 & 0.359 & 0.419 & 0.22 \\\\ -0.464 & -0.242 & 0.712 & 0.166 & 0.438\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.329 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.311 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.591 & 0.31 & 0.745 \\\\ -0.558 & -0.823 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.582 & 0.475 & -0.66\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.329,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=0.9,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.311.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈92.4%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈99.2%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $92.4%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $99.2%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/e2/e2af29d0509f7ae3e3826cd35d3190b478e9c2415c00fc0e3798800d33381c59.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95%$ of the variance." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9dfae55b-dcf9-5c8f-99dd-37e10549a26b" "answer-field-0c6296bc-3942-5538-b3c5-b580c49e804f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9dfae55b-dcf9-5c8f-99dd-37e10549a26b" :answer-field/id #uuid "9dfae55b-dcf9-5c8f-99dd-37e10549a26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a3fa802e-1a7c-5131-9274-0f3fd251222c" "answer-abe5576c-3f57-582e-a2f6-b681ef2c6ece" "answer-ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" "answer-cfa5d643-96f3-5f51-b2d9-8f6ea8ab456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3fa802e-1a7c-5131-9274-0f3fd251222c" :answer/id #uuid "a3fa802e-1a7c-5131-9274-0f3fd251222c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abe5576c-3f57-582e-a2f6-b681ef2c6ece" :answer/id #uuid "abe5576c-3f57-582e-a2f6-b681ef2c6ece" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" :answer/id #uuid "ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfa5d643-96f3-5f51-b2d9-8f6ea8ab456c" :answer/id #uuid "cfa5d643-96f3-5f51-b2d9-8f6ea8ab456c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c6296bc-3942-5538-b3c5-b580c49e804f" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6296bc-3942-5538-b3c5-b580c49e804f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1aa5625e-af4e-5cb0-be77-41320df40a0b" "answer-bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" "answer-29775c65-a127-562d-905b-ce5a0a1506c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aa5625e-af4e-5cb0-be77-41320df40a0b" :answer/id #uuid "1aa5625e-af4e-5cb0-be77-41320df40a0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" :answer/id #uuid "bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29775c65-a127-562d-905b-ce5a0a1506c8" :answer/id #uuid "29775c65-a127-562d-905b-ce5a0a1506c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" :question/id #uuid "439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" :question/math-academy-id "q-355908" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.36 & 1.7 & 0.25 & 1.05 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.33 & -1.37 & -0.53 \\\\ -0.51 & -0.23 & 0.05 & -0.61 \\\\ -1.18 & -0.96 & -0.32 & -1.01 \\\\ 1.25 & -0.18 & 1.4 & 1.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.477 & 0.704 & -0.239 & 0.135 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.291 & -0.357 & -0.764 & 0.049 & 0.449 \\\\ -0.217 & 0.106 & 0.24 & -0.83 & 0.442 \\\\ -0.55 & 0.135 & 0.445 & 0.529 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.582 & -0.589 & 0.323 & 0.104 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.041 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.967 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.671 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.268 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.356 & -0.757 & -0.46 & -0.298 \\\\ 0.454 & 0.644 & -0.416 & -0.454 \\\\ 0.493 & -0.1 & 0.783 & -0.366 \\\\ 0.652 & 0.041 & -0.052 & 0.755\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.041,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.967,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.671,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.268.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈57.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈82.1%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈99.6%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $57.9%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.1%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $99.6%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb43d1adcfa48f0fd66cd73ea05b40a9e937399457ba8614f380502c1c632dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c994f1c-25ce-53c1-b2bc-ad51547e017a" "answer-field-8575b8f8-052d-504c-a3e1-9051091e1d1f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c994f1c-25ce-53c1-b2bc-ad51547e017a" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c994f1c-25ce-53c1-b2bc-ad51547e017a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e00e59c-c2e1-5d95-88f6-75a0287bc55a" "answer-8dd94c47-0c6d-5b9d-a0a0-01f30dc5aa0b" "answer-64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" "answer-359ded72-a1d6-5847-8db5-fe5d7574386d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e00e59c-c2e1-5d95-88f6-75a0287bc55a" :answer/id #uuid "2e00e59c-c2e1-5d95-88f6-75a0287bc55a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dd94c47-0c6d-5b9d-a0a0-01f30dc5aa0b" :answer/id #uuid "8dd94c47-0c6d-5b9d-a0a0-01f30dc5aa0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" :answer/id #uuid "64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-359ded72-a1d6-5847-8db5-fe5d7574386d" :answer/id #uuid "359ded72-a1d6-5847-8db5-fe5d7574386d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8575b8f8-052d-504c-a3e1-9051091e1d1f" :answer-field/id #uuid "8575b8f8-052d-504c-a3e1-9051091e1d1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-13b9077d-bc80-505b-8b3d-48730306347d" "answer-162330ce-ca49-5e79-89b6-d89a13587017" "answer-c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" "answer-beb61f16-161b-5af3-980b-30635c4dc9c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13b9077d-bc80-505b-8b3d-48730306347d" :answer/id #uuid "13b9077d-bc80-505b-8b3d-48730306347d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-162330ce-ca49-5e79-89b6-d89a13587017" :answer/id #uuid "162330ce-ca49-5e79-89b6-d89a13587017" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" :answer/id #uuid "c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-beb61f16-161b-5af3-980b-30635c4dc9c7" :answer/id #uuid "beb61f16-161b-5af3-980b-30635c4dc9c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c6804c35-f8e1-5715-b170-24ee05ad3186" :question/id #uuid "c6804c35-f8e1-5715-b170-24ee05ad3186" :question/math-academy-id "q-355842" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.42 & -0.33 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.94 & 1.07 & -0.08 \\\\ 1.11 & 0.12 & 1.71 \\\\ -0.79 & -1.51 & -0.9 \\\\ 1.04 & 0.66 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.244 & 0.013 & 0.022 & -0.603 & 0.759 \\\\ -0.051 & -0.763 & 0.397 & 0.405 & 0.307 \\\\ 0.671 & 0.439 & 0.394 & 0.235 & 0.383 \\\\ -0.644 & 0.452 & 0.021 & 0.57 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.272 & -0.144 & -0.828 & 0.305 & 0.355\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.730 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.751 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.210 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.617 & 0.395 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.461 & -0.882 & -0.095 \\\\ 0.638 & 0.255 & 0.727\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.730,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.751,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.210.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈62.2%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈87.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $62.2%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $87.8%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5cc8cae6fac8d24e20c045990d39d088a34df26402a7f1e5137070579de03.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $85%$ of the variance." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0104538f-abdf-5164-878b-46a18f047fce" "answer-field-b4176f7b-81bc-51fd-bd74-a22248865cb9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0104538f-abdf-5164-878b-46a18f047fce" :answer-field/id #uuid "0104538f-abdf-5164-878b-46a18f047fce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" :answer-field/choices ["answer-780b7db4-aa11-5ca7-beff-a5f093f3ef15" "answer-8b461c20-1050-5d9a-91cd-8bc9ef709386" "answer-cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" "answer-b8fe6545-9475-5823-b8b7-1c317fcf1b30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-780b7db4-aa11-5ca7-beff-a5f093f3ef15" :answer/id #uuid "780b7db4-aa11-5ca7-beff-a5f093f3ef15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b461c20-1050-5d9a-91cd-8bc9ef709386" :answer/id #uuid "8b461c20-1050-5d9a-91cd-8bc9ef709386" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" :answer/id #uuid "cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8fe6545-9475-5823-b8b7-1c317fcf1b30" :answer/id #uuid "b8fe6545-9475-5823-b8b7-1c317fcf1b30" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4176f7b-81bc-51fd-bd74-a22248865cb9" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4176f7b-81bc-51fd-bd74-a22248865cb9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e85b414d-9378-5c62-830c-20363052d009" "answer-32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" "answer-2646603c-69ca-578d-954e-dddbf2e2cb50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e85b414d-9378-5c62-830c-20363052d009" :answer/id #uuid "e85b414d-9378-5c62-830c-20363052d009" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" :answer/id #uuid "32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2646603c-69ca-578d-954e-dddbf2e2cb50" :answer/id #uuid "2646603c-69ca-578d-954e-dddbf2e2cb50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d455f69c-b6d0-5a22-baea-4655fa27f3c7" :question/id #uuid "d455f69c-b6d0-5a22-baea-4655fa27f3c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-355960" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2.506 & 4.309 & 6.738 \\\\ 0.552 & 4.174 & 1.558 \\\\ 0.175 & 3.635 & 2.487 \\\\ 1.127 & 4.541 & 5.878 \\\\ 4.088 & 4.508 & 5.688\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}-1.35 & 0.21 & 0.99 \\\\ -0.06 & -0.16 & -1.27 \\\\ -0.22 & -1.63 & -0.86 \\\\ 0.19 & 0.84 & 0.61 \\\\ 1.44 & 0.75 & 0.53\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.155 & -0.805 & -0.027 & 0.027 & 0.572 \\\\ -0.358 & 0.242 & 0.758 & 0.142 & 0.467 \\\\ -0.675 & 0.055 & -0.551 & 0.438 & 0.214 \\\\ 0.396 & -0.030 & 0.144 & 0.887 & -0.184 \\\\ 0.485 & 0.538 & -0.317 & -0.019 & 0.612\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.635 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.034 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.940 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.279 & 0.899 & -0.337 \\\\ 0.716 & 0.039 & 0.697 \\\\ 0.640 & -0.435 & -0.633\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=[\\begin{aligned}-1.35 & 0.21 & 0.99 \\\\ -0.06 & -0.16 & -1.27 \\\\ -0.22 & -1.63 & -0.86 \\\\ 0.19 & 0.84 & 0.61 \\\\ 1.44 & 0.75 & 0.53\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.155 & -0.805 & -0.027 & 0.027 & 0.572 \\\\ -0.358 & 0.242 & 0.758 & 0.142 & 0.467 \\\\ -0.675 & 0.055 & -0.551 & 0.438 & 0.214 \\\\ 0.396 & -0.030 & 0.144 & 0.887 & -0.184 \\\\ 0.485 & 0.538 & -0.317 & -0.019 & 0.612\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.635 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.034 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.940 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.279 & 0.899 & -0.337 \\\\ 0.716 & 0.039 & 0.697 \\\\ 0.640 & -0.435 & -0.633\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.635}^{2}}{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}+{0.940}^{2}}≈58.0%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}}{{2.635}^{2}+{2.034}^{2}+{0.940}^{2}}≈92.6%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $58.0%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $92.6%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e39ffb1-f164-5926-be82-036b34a8c594" "answer-field-928d08ff-b7e8-54ee-ae1b-df3fcba70f7b" "answer-field-c23ad7bc-38a5-5932-a9d8-1009a5f433b3" "answer-field-defd6918-4c92-5ef4-b938-cc21b112d088" "answer-field-e3127644-8b0b-57d9-bdea-6ebb18e85e3a" "answer-field-94c0eef6-b225-5e5c-9bbe-1737197f620e" "answer-field-5986ba65-5099-53e3-88f5-f5e4fd954080" "answer-field-23a3adc0-daa9-5a27-927c-6db6359a3b42" "answer-field-b50874a2-e3a7-5329-bf0c-106fdd139ec0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e39ffb1-f164-5926-be82-036b34a8c594" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e39ffb1-f164-5926-be82-036b34a8c594" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-5f522d33-447f-5a33-87f8-afec283b06d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e21e9b71-d5fe-5002-bbf9-c83a592a7e5c" "answer-5f522d33-447f-5a33-87f8-afec283b06d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e21e9b71-d5fe-5002-bbf9-c83a592a7e5c" :answer/id #uuid "e21e9b71-d5fe-5002-bbf9-c83a592a7e5c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the datapoint" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f522d33-447f-5a33-87f8-afec283b06d7" :answer/id #uuid "5f522d33-447f-5a33-87f8-afec283b06d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-928d08ff-b7e8-54ee-ae1b-df3fcba70f7b" :answer-field/id #uuid "928d08ff-b7e8-54ee-ae1b-df3fcba70f7b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-7b14cb21-f592-50e6-89dd-a0523693c954" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3a51d53-8591-5fb3-a416-1f8899a46c81" "answer-7b14cb21-f592-50e6-89dd-a0523693c954"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3a51d53-8591-5fb3-a416-1f8899a46c81" :answer/id #uuid "b3a51d53-8591-5fb3-a4